    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is pretty good for air tightness, however there are some nuissance areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlet in the master bedroom exterior wall, the weather stripping along the two doors, the old vent pipe for the basement bathroom exhaust fan, and the wire/pipe penetrations around the electrical panel in the basement. 



Air leakage was also observed through the kitchen rangehood fan and bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



Although the estimated savings below are relatively low, this will help with overall comfort within the home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 10&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of Extruded polystyrene insulation.



Please note, the savings below include increasing the insulation of the main stud walls (ie. not including any basement upgrades). 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R20 of Extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 610&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;20.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was estimated to have 8 inches (R28) of blown cellulose insulation. I have modelled adding an additional 6 inches (R22) blown cellulose insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R22) blown cellulose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete plus 2x3 studs with 2 inches (R6) of fiberglass batt insulation . I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation.



As the header spaces are uneven with the concrete height, using spray foam may simplify insulating these areas. Using spray foam will also help with air sealing in tough areas along the header connections. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;35.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your basement windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 5 basement windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home, this is most likely why you noticed moisture/ice build up along the older windows. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV could be tied into the existing furnace duct work to minimize installation requirements. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 20&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Option 1:&lt;/b&gt; Direct Vent Oil, 87% AFUE Furnace



Your current oil fired furnace was assumed to be rated at 83% Steady State efficiency. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to a direct vented oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a direct vent oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 630&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;21.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates for an Oil fired furnace below 92% AFUE. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Option 2:&lt;/b&gt; Condensing Propane, 95% AFUE Furnace



I have also modeled an upgrade to a sealed combustion propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;36.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Boiler as an Option&lt;/b&gt;



As you mentioned the two front rooms upstairs are generally colder compared to the rest of the home, a boiler may be a potential route to consider. As the thermostat is located in the 'main' portion of the home, the front two rooms are seperated from that thermostat... as well, when the two front rooms cool down from the front door opening and closing, the thermostat still 'thinks' the house is up to the appropriate temperature. 



With a boiler, two heating loops can be used to heat each section of the home. The main portion of the home can be heated using a fan coil system off the boiler, which will allow the existing duct work to be used, similar to a furnace. The front two rooms however could have a boiler loop installed on a seperate thermostat, giving you more control on the tempature of those rooms. 



The savings by installing a boiler would be very similar to the furnace savings above, however there would be a bit more electricy required to operate this design off a boiler. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;



If you decide to install a pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 



By installing an 80% efficient pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 800 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy saving, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt;, 1/3 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

